f 17592186045417] [17592186443980 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443980 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443980 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186443938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443980 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dcc854e9-e298-54b3-acbf-549aa127409d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443981 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443981 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443981 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186443931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443981 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f7768791-4d77-54d9-9010-4c03c60e2cb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443982 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443982 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443982 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443982 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443982 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443970 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443982 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443982 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443982 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186443928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443982 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd178739-0906-50de-a480-2154dbc44ed0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443983 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443974 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443983 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443983 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443983 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-4Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.3.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443983 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1634e1ab-c584-5b67-9e78-d7bac12ef42f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443983 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)((1-4Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =2(1-4Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-4Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-4Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-4Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-4Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443984 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda =18$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $Δx=0.04$\n\nII. $Δx=0.1$\n\nIII. $Δx=0.15$\n\nIV. $Δx=0.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f49a9a1-5ba0-5f85-aec8-d5a800fd601f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443984 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler’s method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region\n$0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =18,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <18Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{18} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.111….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.04$ *satisfies* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.1$ *satisfies* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.15$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality.\n\nHence, Euler’s method is unstable for the step sizes $Δx=0.15$ and $Δx=0.2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443985 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443985 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443985 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443985 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following values is Euler's method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=2.4$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=0.9$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.8$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443985 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28574ce8-fd0a-501f-b680-ed91403e93fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443985 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.4,$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.9,$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.8,$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is **unstable** for the values $\\lambda Δx=2.4,$ and $\\lambda Δx=2.8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443986 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443986 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443986 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=4,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-2Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-2Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443986 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a221524-8030-56ce-9632-c32e19b2687d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443986 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=4,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=4.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-2{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-2Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-2Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-2Δx)((1-2Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-2Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-2Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =4(1-2Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-2Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-2Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-2Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-2Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <2Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443987 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443987 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(2Δx)=\\frac{1}{1-2Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1-2Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.30.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "639c33be-3d9f-5ed3-823e-9a34ba608c41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443987 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-2{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+2{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+2Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(2Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since $1+2Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+2Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.30.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443988 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=\\frac{7}{3},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{11}{5},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.3.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5501ecd-a534-5143-a2fa-d60083b2450a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443988 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{7}{3},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{11}{5}y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{11}{5}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{11}{5}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)((1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}{(1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{11}{5}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{11}{5}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{11}{5}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{10}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is false.\n\n- Since $Δx=1$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443989 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443989 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443989 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443989 #edb/ref 1071 "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. The stability function is $R(5Δx)=1-5Δx+\\frac{25}{2}(Δx)^{2}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443989 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "acf9ba03-3b41-5ee5-9008-523784ef9cff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443989 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}-5({y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-10{y}_{n}+25{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-5Δx+\\frac{25}{2}(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=1-5Δx+\\frac{25}{2}(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-5Δx+\\frac{25}{2}(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|\\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ \\frac{25}{2}{(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}<Δx-\\frac{1}{5} & <\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it does not satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333983" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443990 #edb/ref 1071 "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =8,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(8Δx)=1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.6.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ad84f498-bd85-5632-acda-fbc8df689a1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443990 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-8y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-8{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-8{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-8{y}_{n}-8({y}_{n}-8{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-16{y}_{n}+64{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-8{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+32{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(8Δx)=1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(8Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-8Δx+32(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 32{(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{8})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{8}<Δx-\\frac{1}{8} & <\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, it does *not* satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.6,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443975 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334467" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443991 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following values is the Trapezoidal Method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.45$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.7$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=3.2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=9.4$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the Trapezoidal Method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{\\,1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1d62ba0-0114-5483-9f12-c0fecd4e79bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443991 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=5,\\,x\\ge 0,\\text{ }\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx)\\,{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the trapezoidal method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the trapezoidal method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.45,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.45)| & =|\\frac{1-0.45/2}{1+0.45/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.775}{1.225}| \\\\  & =0.626…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.7)| & =|\\frac{1-1.7/2}{1+1.7/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.15}{1.85}| \\\\  & =0.0810…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.2)| & =|\\frac{1-3.2/2}{1+3.2/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-0.6}{2.6}| \\\\  & =0.2307…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=9.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(9.4)| & =|\\frac{1-9.4/2}{1+9.4/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-3.7}{5.7}| \\\\  & =0.6491…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “I, II, III, and IV”.\n\nNote that, since the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443992 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443992 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334716" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443992 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443992 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.9$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.6$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf3c4ec0-335d-5e9d-91d7-9dd1f9e287ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443992 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|1-0.8+\\frac{(0.8)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{13}{25}| \\\\  & =0.52<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|1-1.2+\\frac{(1.2)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{13}{25}| \\\\  & =0.52<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.9)| & =|1-1.9+\\frac{(1.9)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{181}{200}| \\\\  & =0.905<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.6)| & =|1-2.6+\\frac{(2.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{89}{50}| \\\\  & =1.78>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the modified Euler method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443993 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda =5$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $Δx=0.1$\n\nII. $Δx=0.25$\n\nIII. $Δx=0.3$\n\nIV. $Δx=1.5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb08f354-4c44-52a2-9c76-c85c4970a5f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443993 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =5,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <5Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.1$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.3$ satisfies the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=1.5$ doesn't satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler’s method is stable for step sizes $Δx=0.1,$ $Δx=0.25,$ and $Δx=0.3.$\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
